#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176044 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf8d806a-b232-5ddd-ad0b-3af70f244194" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Congruent Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25891548-1783-5b6a-b98e-416aa7ad7ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25891548-1783-5b6a-b98e-416aa7ad7ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496738} :answer-field/id #uuid "6482d0d5-ec8d-54c5-8c10-350eede2f3ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cbcdbef5-9dc5-5670-8a90-267993182080" :question/math-academy-id "q-166838" :question/problem "![](images/04/04676bef8cc37d05e404dc03211968ebaf39f7adbfc54a4a2cb4d5f8cdc3f1a9.png)\n\nA circular cake is cut into three slices, as shown above. Given that $m∠A=23z+{4}^{∘},$ $m∠B=12z+{6}^{∘},$ and $m∠C=15z,$ then$\\,z=$\n{{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the entire cake corresponds to the full angle of ${360}^{∘}.$\n\nBy the angle addition postulate,\n\n$$\nm∠A+m∠B+m∠C={360}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting the expressions for $m∠A,$ $m∠B,$ and $m∠C,$ and solving for $z,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ (23z+{4}^{∘})+(12z+{6}^{∘})+(15z) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 50z+{10}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 50z & ={350}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={7}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea607a9-7512-5062-b4ae-c53e0f653efd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Angle Type From a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9cae770-6109-54d3-b8a4-8df741927514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9cae770-6109-54d3-b8a4-8df741927514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497194} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6295df7-9006-5cdd-b997-240187450a04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16a5b9ce-8ad5-5f67-b7e3-0fec61fbfd8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-166839" :question/problem "Which slice ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) of the cake slices from part 1 corresponds to a right angle?\n\nSlice$\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that $z={7}^{∘}.$ \n\nWe substitute it into the expression for angles and obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =23({7}^{∘})+{4}^{∘}={165}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & =12({7}^{∘})+{6}^{∘}={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & =15({7}^{∘})={105}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, slice $B$ corresponds to a right angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110619 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff2f965-b406-5c9a-bd41-73ccdce951eb" :knowledge-point/title "Cross-Multiplying with One Binomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c1738da-f777-5f76-9b75-c6e7cc26ee58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c1738da-f777-5f76-9b75-c6e7cc26ee58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496755} :answer-field/id #uuid "2216ef52-715e-5e56-a65f-58cd2ceb2ca9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67306212-e963-567c-a3f1-8493d029556a" :question/math-academy-id "q-166840" :question/problem "How many cakes can the pastry shop produce with $9$ packs of chocolate?\n\nThe pastry shop can produce$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$cakes." :question/worked-solution "To produce $x$ cakes, the pastry shop uses $(\\frac{5x+3}{2})$ packs of chocolate. So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+3}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nWe need to solve this linear equation for $x.$\nFirst, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x+3}{2} & =\\frac{9}{1} \\\\ (5x+3)\\cdot 1 & =9\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x+3)\\cdot 1 & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\ 5x+3 & =18 \\\\ 5x+3-3 & =18-3 \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the pastry shop can produce $3$ cakes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110620 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e880edeb-82ad-5943-a846-dc3246e3e3bd" :knowledge-point/title "Cross-Multiplying with Two Binomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2a3a79c-7ba3-5ecb-b30e-0a41e9cc0d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2a3a79c-7ba3-5ecb-b30e-0a41e9cc0d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497098} :answer-field/id #uuid "2504adaf-a330-523d-8d22-11511f8bc70d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b477291d-9f4b-5add-8cfc-437d184095c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-166841" :question/problem "How many cakes does the pastry shop produce if they use equal numbers of packs of flour and butter?\n\nThe pastry shop produces$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$cakes." :question/worked-solution "To produce $x$ cakes, the pastry shop uses the following amounts:\n\n* $\\frac{4x+1}{6}$ packs of butter\n\n* $\\frac{13x+5}{20}$ packs of flour\n\nIf the pastry shop uses the same number of packs of butter and flour, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x+1}{6}=\\frac{13x+5}{20}.\n$$\n\nWe need to solve this linear equation for $x.$ First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x+1}{6} & =\\frac{13x+5}{20} \\\\ (4x+1)\\cdot 20 & =6\\cdot (13x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4x+1)\\cdot 20 & =6\\cdot (13x+5) \\\\ 80x+20 & =78x+30 \\\\ 80x+20-78x & =78x+30-78x \\\\ 2x+20 & =30 \\\\ 2x+20-20 & =30-20 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $x=5$ cakes are produced when the pastry shop uses the same number of packs of butter and flour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073752 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e79a91b-c479-53da-9f06-59c082fa4187" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "951ed025-e4b5-517b-830a-a3da54b9c6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "951ed025-e4b5-517b-830a-a3da54b9c6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496968} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c523c48-ec60-5fa0-9eec-5d07498f1571" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497224} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eebbe5cc-91ee-5b03-9257-cc5f956ce523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eebbe5cc-91ee-5b03-9257-cc5f956ce523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497171} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb7943f0-b18a-5a1c-9e1c-77a8a3b53234" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ededc7bf-b310-5714-a047-930df525362b" :question/math-academy-id "q-166843" :question/problem "The pastry shop's total cost and revenue per month (in dollars) from selling cakes can be modeled by the equations $y=1\\,600+5n$ and $y=64+17n,$ respectively, where $n$ is the number of cakes sold. \n\nSolve the system below using the substitution method to find the exact number of cakes needed to break even and the revenue made.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1\\,600+5n \\\\ y=64+17n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pastry shop breaks even when$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$cakes are produced. \n\nThe revenue made when selling this number of cakes is$\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,$dollars." :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that $y$ is isolated in the first equation. Therefore, we can replace $y$ with $1\\,600+5n$ in the second equation and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =64+17n \\\\ 1\\,600+5n & =64+17n \\\\ 1\\,600+5n-64 & =64+17n-64 \\\\ 1\\,536+5n & =17n \\\\ 1\\,536+5n-5n & =17n-5n \\\\ 1\\,536 & =12n \\\\ \\frac{1\\,536}{12} & =\\frac{12n}{12} \\\\ 128 & =n \\\\ n & =128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $n=128$ into $y=64+17n$, then we can find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=64+17(128)=2\\,240\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solution to the system of equations is $n=128$ and $y=2\\,240.$\nTherefore, the pastry shop breaks even when $128$ units are produced, and the revenue made when selling this number of units is $$2\\,240.$"}]}
